1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,950
A carrier wave is a radio signal that carries, by itself, absolutely nothing.

2
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:11,836
It's a steady radio-frequency signal at a single frequency — the same cycle,

3
00:00:11,836 --> 00:00:15,051
repeated forever, with no voice and no data in it.

4
00:00:15,051 --> 00:00:19,358
And yet it's the foundation of every transmission you'll ever tune.

5
00:00:19,358 --> 00:00:22,701
The carrier is the vehicle; the message rides on it.

6
00:00:23,151 --> 00:00:24,544
Here's the definition.

7
00:00:24,544 --> 00:00:29,166
A carrier conveys no information on its own — it becomes useful only when

8
00:00:29,166 --> 00:00:34,041
modulation varies one of its properties, its amplitude, its frequency, or its

9
00:00:34,041 --> 00:00:35,941
phase, in step with a message.

10
00:00:35,941 --> 00:00:40,563
When you say a station is "on" some frequency, you're naming its carrier.

11
00:00:40,563 --> 00:00:45,628
Tuning a receiver means picking one carrier out of the crowd — which is why "the

12
00:00:45,628 --> 00:00:49,997
frequency of a station" and "its carrier" mean nearly the same thing.

13
00:00:50,447 --> 00:00:53,043
Why bother with a wave that says nothing?

14
00:00:53,043 --> 00:00:58,109
Think of an empty delivery truck: it moves no cargo, but it's the thing that can

15
00:00:58,109 --> 00:00:58,995
make the trip.

16
00:00:58,995 --> 00:01:03,934
Your voice can't make the trip itself — an antenna is only efficient at a size

17
00:01:03,934 --> 00:01:09,127
comparable to the wavelength, and baseband audio, a few kilohertz, has wavelengths

18
00:01:09,127 --> 00:01:10,520
of tens of kilometres.

19
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:11,976
Impractical to radiate.

20
00:01:11,976 --> 00:01:16,662
Shift the message up onto a high-frequency carrier, and it radiates from a

21
00:01:16,662 --> 00:01:17,865
reasonable antenna.

22
00:01:17,865 --> 00:01:22,931
And there's a second win: carriers let many stations share the spectrum, each on

23
00:01:22,931 --> 00:01:28,123
its own carrier frequency with a guard band between them, and a receiver picks one

24
00:01:28,123 --> 00:01:29,326
out just by tuning.

25
00:01:29,776 --> 00:01:32,192
Now watch what the message does to it.

26
00:01:32,192 --> 00:01:37,342
A pure, unmodulated carrier is a single sinusoid, so in the frequency domain it's

27
00:01:37,342 --> 00:01:41,283
one narrow spike — all of its energy at exactly one frequency.

28
00:01:41,283 --> 00:01:46,369
The moment it's modulated, that energy spreads: the message frequencies mix with

29
00:01:46,369 --> 00:01:51,455
the carrier and produce sidebands — mirror-image bands of energy above and below

30
00:01:51,455 --> 00:01:52,853
the carrier frequency.

31
00:01:52,853 --> 00:01:58,130
The span those sidebands occupy is essentially the signal's bandwidth, and it grows

32
00:01:58,130 --> 00:02:00,800
with the rate and depth of the modulation.

33
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:06,077
A voice AM signal spreads a few kilohertz either side of its carrier; a wideband FM

34
00:02:06,077 --> 00:02:08,174
broadcast, hundreds of kilohertz.

35
00:02:08,624 --> 00:02:11,073
You can see all of this on a waterfall.

36
00:02:11,073 --> 00:02:14,777
An idle transmitter is a thin bright line — a bare carrier.

37
00:02:14,777 --> 00:02:18,105
Key it up, and the line blooms into a modulated band.

38
00:02:18,105 --> 00:02:22,876
That presence-of-carrier test is also the simplest squelch there is: carrier

39
00:02:22,876 --> 00:02:27,899
squelch opens the audio whenever a strong enough carrier shows up, regardless of

40
00:02:27,899 --> 00:02:28,715
what's on it.

41
00:02:29,165 --> 00:02:32,112
In GopherTrunk you'll meet one famous impostor.

42
00:02:32,112 --> 00:02:37,192
After the SDR mixes your chosen slice of spectrum down toward baseband, the tuned

43
00:02:37,192 --> 00:02:42,334
carrier lands near zero frequency — and a residual carrier or DC offset sitting at

44
00:02:42,334 --> 00:02:47,350
exactly zero paints the familiar bright "DC spike" in the centre of the display.

45
00:02:47,350 --> 00:02:49,671
It's an artifact, not a real station.

46
00:02:49,671 --> 00:02:54,625
The real carriers matter more: GopherTrunk locks onto each channel's carrier to

47
00:02:54,625 --> 00:02:59,830
establish a phase and frequency reference, and the digital voice systems it decodes

48
00:02:59,830 --> 00:03:04,909
keep the carrier present and modulate its phase — so accurate carrier tracking is

49
00:03:04,909 --> 00:03:07,480
the foundation of the whole decode chain.

50
00:03:07,930 --> 00:03:12,929
So: a carrier is a steady wave that exists to be changed — modulation spreads it

51
00:03:12,929 --> 00:03:17,865
into sidebands that carry the message, tuning means choosing a carrier, and the

52
00:03:17,865 --> 00:03:21,489
bright spike in the middle of your SDR's screen isn't one.

53
00:03:21,489 --> 00:03:23,176
Full write-up linked below.

